A Simple Game House Application — COMP3234 / ELEC3443
====================================================

Requirements and files
----------------------
Python 3.8 or later; standard library only, with no pip dependencies.
Run the terminal client on Linux or macOS (POSIX). Its single-threaded input
loop uses select() on both standard input and the TCP socket; Windows console
input does not support this interface. A terminal, pipe, or redirected file
can supply client input.

  GameServer.py  Multi-client TCP server and game/room state machine.
  GameClient.py  Single-threaded interactive TCP client.
  README         Setup, protocol, implementation, and testing instructions.

Running
-------
Create a separate UserInfo.txt containing one username:password per line:

  Jacky:12345
  Bob:54321
  Michael:abcde

These are demonstration accounts, not real credentials. Usernames/passwords
must be nonempty and contain no whitespace; usernames must not contain ':'.
Blank lines are ignored; malformed entries and duplicate usernames are rejected
at startup. Passwords may contain ':'. The server loads the file once at startup.

Terminal 1:
  python3 GameServer.py 22222 UserInfo.txt

Terminal 2:
  python3 GameClient.py localhost 22222

Terminal 3 (and optionally Terminal 4):
  python3 GameClient.py localhost 22222

Use a different demonstration account for each client. Enter the username and
password after the exact prompts; failed authentication repeats both prompts.
The server has 10 rooms, numbered 1 through 10 (NUM_ROOMS in GameServer.py).
It listens on all IPv4 interfaces. Both programs validate argument counts and
ports (1–65535), and report startup errors without a user-facing traceback.

Commands and example interaction
--------------------------------
After authentication, type a hall command:

  /list       Display room counts: 3001 10 <ten occupancy counts>.
  /enter N    Enter room N. First player waits; the second starts the game.
  /exit       Receive 4001 Bye bye and close the client.

For example, Jacky types /list and /enter 1, then receives 3011 Wait. Bob types
/enter 1; both receive 3012 Game started. Please guess true or false. Each then
types just true or false; the client sends /guess true or /guess false. After
submitting a valid guess, wait for the other player's guess and the result.
Both players automatically return to the hall after a result.

If both guess true, or both guess false, both receive 3023 The result is a tie.
If their guesses differ, the server chooses a random Boolean: exactly one
receives 3021 You are the winner and the other receives 3022 You lost this game.
Typing an invalid guess receives 4002 Unrecognized message and permits retry.
Do not include /guess when typing a guess into this client.

A third player entering an active room receives 3013 The room is full and stays
in the hall. Only the hall permits /list, /enter and /exit. Waiting players send
nothing; playing players may submit one valid guess. Malformed or out-of-state
commands receive exactly 4002 Unrecognized message without changing game state.
The client prints server replies without additional hall/guess prompts.

Wire protocol and assumptions
-----------------------------
Transport is IPv4 TCP. Each command and response is a UTF-8 line terminated by
'\n'; the delimiter is not part of the specified message text. CRLF is accepted.
There is no JSON, length prefix, greeting, heartbeat, or extra protocol message.
When testing with a raw socket, append '\n' to every command and read complete
lines: TCP can split one message or combine several messages in a single recv().
Unterminated bytes are buffered, not guessed to be a complete command.

Commands are case-sensitive whitespace-separated tokens. /login requires
exactly a username and password; /enter requires one ASCII integer in range;
/guess accepts only the exact lowercase tokens true and false. Incoming command
lines are limited to 4096 bytes. Invalid UTF-8 and oversized lines receive 4002;
an oversized line is discarded through its newline before parsing resumes.
Accounts are not globally reserved: the assignment assumes that the same user
does not log in simultaneously. Session identity is the connection, not username.
Normal /exit is only supported in the hall, as specified. TCP/passwords are
unencrypted, as in the assignment; use demonstration credentials.

Implementation and synchronization
----------------------------------
The main server thread accepts connections and starts one new handler thread
for each new client socket. A Session holds the socket, username, state, room
index, guess, and outgoing queue. Rooms contain Session objects. States are
AUTHENTICATING, IN_HALL, WAITING, PLAYING, and DISCONNECTED.

One threading.Lock protects all room membership and session/game state. /list
reads its entire snapshot under this lock. Entry, guess validation, normal game
resolution, and disconnect cleanup update state atomically under the same lock.
Completion clears both guesses, room indices, and membership, returning both
players to the hall before releasing the lock. A second thread therefore cannot
resolve the same game or remove its players a second time.

Responses are queued while holding the state lock. Only the socket's owning
handler calls sendall(), outside that lock. FIFO queues preserve Wait/Start/
Result ordering and prevent concurrent writes from interleaving. No application
lock is held during network I/O, socket closure, or thread joins. A 0.2-second
socket timeout lets idle handlers service queued notifications; idle receive
timeouts do not disconnect players. A failed or timed-out send is a connection
failure and triggers that handler's cleanup.

Abnormal disconnections
-----------------------
Every handler uses try/except/finally and one idempotent cleanup function. EOF,
reset, send failure, or an unexpected handler exception removes the session.
A waiting player's room becomes empty. If a player leaves an unresolved game,
cleanup clears the room and queues 3021 You are the winner for the remaining
player, who returns to the hall immediately. Concurrent cleanup cannot award a
second result. A failed result delivery cannot reopen an already completed game.
The server continues accepting clients after an individual connection fails.

The client monitors the server socket even while awaiting keyboard input. EOF
and socket errors are reported gracefully, including during authentication,
waiting, and playing. A guess sent just as the opponent disconnects may be
rejected after the win notification; this late 4002 is printed without undoing
the return to the hall. Delayed errors also cannot enable a second valid guess.
Ctrl-C closes the client socket immediately; input EOF closes it when the
current exchange permits keyboard input. Ctrl-C on the server shuts down
active connections. Both sides enable TCP keepalive and
attempt to tune it to a 15-second idle period, 5-second interval and 3 probes.
Silent network loss is detectable only when TCP reports failure; exact timing
depends on OS support and retransmission behavior. No application timeout
mistakes an otherwise healthy, idle waiting player for a disconnected player.

Testing
-------
Syntax check without creating __pycache__:
  python3 -c "import ast,pathlib; [ast.parse(pathlib.Path(p).read_text()) for p in ('GameServer.py','GameClient.py')]; print('Syntax OK')"

Use the terminals above to check:
1. Wrong then correct credentials; /list initially shows ten zero counts.
2. Jacky enters room 1: Wait. Bob's /list shows one occupant; Bob enters room 1:
   both start. Before either guesses, Michael's /list shows two occupants and
   /enter 1 returns room full.
3. Both type true: tie. /list shows room 1 empty. Repeat with both false, then
   with different guesses to see exactly one winner and one loser.
4. In the hall try /enter 0, /enter -1, /enter 11, /enter abc, /enter, /enter 1 2,
   /list extra, /exit now, garbage, and a blank line: each returns 4002.
5. During a game type maybe, then true: the invalid guess does not count.
6. Close a waiting client with Ctrl-C: its room becomes empty. Restart it.
   Start another game, close one player before guessing or after its guess:
   the survivor wins, can immediately /list, and can reuse the room.
7. Start games in different rooms concurrently. Finish one while the other
   remains active, then reuse rooms repeatedly. Use raw newline-delimited TCP
   clients to exercise simultaneous entries, duplicate guesses, out-of-state
   commands, fragmented commands, and multiple commands in one write.
8. Stop the server while a client is entering credentials, in the hall, waiting,
   entering a guess, and waiting for its result. Each client reports the
   disconnection without a traceback. Also try starting a client without a server.
9. /exit from the hall prints 4001 Bye bye and Client ends and closes cleanly.
10. With a scripted TCP peer, deliver 3021 followed by 4002 while a guess is in
    flight. Verify the client stays usable for /list, another game, and /exit,
    including when the error arrives after a new hall command has been sent.

Submission
----------
Submit only GameServer.py, GameClient.py, and README. Supply your own UserInfo.txt
when running; do not package credentials, tests, logs, caches, or IDE metadata.
Copy these three files to a clean directory and repeat the run instructions.
Replace STUDENTID below with your real student ID:

  zip a1-STUDENTID.zip GameServer.py GameClient.py README

Use a new archive (or check its listing so an older ZIP cannot retain extra files):
  unzip -l a1-STUDENTID.zip
